  "step_2": "The verbal report states: 'unwesentliche unterschiede in der wahrscheinlichkeit zu verlieren, höherer Verlust bei A' (\"insignificant differences in the probability of losing, higher loss in A\"). This indicates that the individual considered the probability of losing (i.e., incurring a non-zero loss) and the magnitude of the loss. They note that the difference in losing probability is small (20% vs 25%), but Lottery A involves a higher loss (-4000 vs -3000). The individual appears to focus on the trade-off between the size of the loss and the likelihood of loss, and implicitly downplays the probability difference while noting the larger loss in A. The justification seems to weigh the severity of the loss more heavily, possibly leading to a preference for B due to its smaller potential loss, despite similar loss probabilities.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness, specifically preferring the more right-skewed lottery (higher probability on unfavorable outcomes). Here, B is more right-skewed (25% loss vs 20%), so the reason would favor B. The individual in the verbal report acknowledges the slightly higher probability of loss in B but dismisses it as 'insignificant', while emphasizing the larger loss in A. This suggests the individual is considering the same elements—probability and magnitude of loss—but is not focusing on or reasoning about the distributional shape (i.e., skewness). Instead, the reasoning appears more aligned with minimizing maximum loss or being sensitive to loss magnitude, not with preferring greater right-skewness. There is no indication that the individual values or prefers the higher probability of loss in B; rather, they seem to disregard it. Thus, while both the reason and the individual consider probabilities and outcomes, the individual does not exhibit the rule of preferring more right-skewed distributions. The justification is inconsistent with the decision reason's preference logic.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
}

{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on comparing the skewness of the two lotteries, specifically preferring the one that is more right-skewed (i.e., assigns higher probability to unfavorable outcomes). To evaluate skewness in this context, we assess the distribution of probabilities over outcomes relative to their favorability. In Lottery A: there is a 90% chance of a -3000 Euro loss (unfavorable) and 10% chance of 0 (neutral/favorable). In Lottery B: 45% chance of -6000 (more unfavorable) and 55% chance of 0 (favorable). Since right-skewness here is defined as higher probability on unfavorable outcomes, Lottery A has 90% on an unfavorable outcome, while Lottery B has only 45% on its (more severe) unfavorable outcome. Thus, Lottery A is more right-skewed. The decision reason would therefore prefer Lottery A. The relevant information (probabilities and outcomes) is available to compute skewness as defined, so the reason can be applied.",
